\ name@key ( -- val ) / name!key ( val -- ): a token joining a frame reference to
\ a key. The part left of the operator is a local, else a defined word; empty
\ means the frame is already on the stack. @ gets, ! sets and drops the frame
\ that ! returns. Chains left to right; only the last step may be a set. The rule
\ applies only to tokens that are not defined words, so a word whose name
\ contains @ or ! keeps its meaning.

{ :price 42 :qty 7 :address { :city "Boston" :zip "02134" } } to row

\ === get through a global ===
row@price . cr         \ 42
row@qty . cr           \ 7
clear

\ === chained gets walk nested frames ===
row@address@city . cr  \ Boston
row@address@zip . cr    \ 02134
clear

\ === the bare form takes the frame from the stack ===
{ :a 1 :b 2 } @b . cr  \ 2
row @price . cr        \ 42
clear

\ === set stores the stack top and leaves nothing ===
99 row!price
row@price . cr         \ 99
.s cr                  \ (empty: ! dropped the frame it returns)
clear

\ === a chain may end in a set ===
"Cambridge" row@address!city
row@address@city . cr  \ Cambridge
clear

\ === locals are the left part inside a definition ===
: price-of | >r | r@price ;
row price-of . cr      \ 99
: bump-price | >r | r@price 1 + r!price ;
row bump-price
row@price . cr         \ 100
clear

\ === one frame reference, several keys ===
: describe | >r | r@qty r@price ;
row describe . . cr    \ 100 7
clear

\ === the key is an operand, not a stack push ===
' price-of see-compiled
' price-of see

\ === a defined word wins over the rule ===
: row@total 77 ;
row@total . cr         \ 77
clear

\ === words whose names contain the operators are untouched ===
[ 1 2 3 ] 0 @i . cr    \ 1
{ :a 1 } :b 9 @or . cr \ 9
[< 1 2 >] 3 set-add! size . cr  \ 3
clear

\ === errors ===
row@missing            \ (no such key)
5 to n
n@price                \ (the left part is not a frame)
row@                   \ (no key after the operator; reported as an unknown word)
nonesuch@price         \ (the left part resolves to nothing)
row!price@qty          \ (only the last step may be a set)
